   "description": "In her early career, Umm Kulthum, accompanied by her father, toured unknown villages in the Nile Delta, performing for local peasants in villages likely near her hometown of Tamay al-Zahira and nearby cities such as Mansoura, Tanta, and Damietta. She performed publicly at mawalid, religious ceremonies, and privately for the Omda (village leaders) of different villages. She walked long distances, often 5–6 km between villages, performing for modest audiences. During this period, she wore very conservative clothing, often similar to male attire, to conceal her gender in these rural and socially conservative contexts. These formative years taught her the value of money, the importance of connecting with rural audiences, and helped her develop both endurance and vocal strength. She personally managed her earnings, traveling with minimal arrangements, and often performed religious chants to appeal to local tastes. Some critics have suggested that this early experience of concealing her gender could have affected her sense of femininity or even her sexual orientation, although this remains unconfirmed.Umm Kulthum began singing at the age of eight, performing for the ma’dhūn (marriage registrar) of her village without payment, as this brought him moral prestige and enhanced his standing in the community. Her first public occasion before a large gathering of villagers was at the wedding of a relative. When she began to sing, her voice captivated hearts—a tender, soulful sound capable of reaching the deepest corners of the spirit.\nThe following day, she was invited to sing at the wedding of a village guard in a nearby estate, where she enthralled the audience from nine in the evening until two in the morning. She received her first payment—ten qirsh—which was the total fee for the entire troupe (Umm Kulthum, her father, and her brother). Five days later, she was invited to perform at an evening event hosted by one of the major grain merchants in al-Senbellawein, for a fee of twenty-five qirsh.\nAfter that, circumstances began to change. A merchant at Abu al-Shaqouq Station decided to organize a ticketed event, charging five qirsh for first class, three qirsh for second class, and free entry for those standing at the back of the tent. Umm Kulthum’s first such concert was a success, earning a fee of one and a half pounds. She then began receiving invitations to perform at weddings in Mansoura, its districts, and surrounding villages.\nUmm Kulthum also recounted that in 1915 she used to ride a donkey, while her father and brother walked beside her. As her fees increased in 1916, each of them was able to ride a donkey. She did not travel except in third-class train cars until 1919, though conductors would sometimes allow them to sit in second class on the condition that she sing throughout the journey. She would sing continuously, stopping only at stations. As her fees rose—along with the rise in cotton prices—her earnings reached eight pounds and later increased to ten pounds.",
