   "works": "The UNESCO Palace Hall in Beirut, Lebanon, was inaugurated in 1948 to host the 3rd International Congress of UNESCO, marking Lebanon’s first major appearance on the global cultural and diplomatic stage after its independence in 1943. Since then, it has served as a premier venue for national and international conferences, lectures, exhibitions, cultural events, and celebrations, hosting around a thousand events annually. The palace was heavily damaged during the 1982 Israeli invasion, which destroyed its theater and equipment, but it was renovated and reopened in March 1998 on the 50th anniversary of the first UNESCO congress held in the East. The interior includes a main theater with 1,200 seats, two eastern and western halls, upper-floor offices and seating, a cafeteria for 300 guests, and two meeting rooms equipped with modern sound and lighting systems accommodating 100 persons each.",
