   "locationInfo": "Originally built in 1903 as the Strand Union Workhouse Infirmary; opened in 1920 as the Institution for Venereal Disease (Sheffield Street Hospital); renamed St Philip’s Hospital in 1952; part of the St Peter’s, St Paul’s and St Philip’s group (“the 3 Ps”); specialized in chronic urological and nephrological diseases with 57 beds; closed in 1992, with nephrology services transferred to the Middlesex Hospital",
   "description": "St Philip’s Hospital served as the London medical institution where Umm Kulthum was admitted during her overseas medical journey in early 1973. Following health deterioration after her final live performance on 7 December 1972, and the onset of symptoms initially believed to be gallbladder-related, she traveled to London accompanied by her husband and treating physician. At St Philip’s Hospital, examinations confirmed that her gallbladder was healthy and that her condition resulted from severe anemia. The visit provided temporary reassurance and reinforced her hope of returning to singing, despite medical recommendations for rest. This admission represents her last confirmed overseas hospital treatment before the terminal phase of her illness.",
