   "eventType": "Musical Performance / Honorary Concert",
   "people": "Umm Kulthum, Ahmad Ramy (poet), Riad al-Sunbati (composer), Ahmad al-Jack (promoter), Sami El Solh (Lebanese Prime Minister), Saber Al-Safah (President of Musicians’ Syndicate of Lebanon), Sheikh Mubarak Abdullah Al-Jaber Al-Sabah (Kuwait)",
   "works": "Ya Zalimni",
   "locationInfo": "The Capitol Hotel, a prominent hotel in mid-20th century Beirut, used for cultural events, political gatherings, and private ceremonies. Venue was erroneously thought to be Ahmad al-Jack’s house but confirmed as The Capitol Hotel.",
   "description": "This performance is often known as the “al-Jack” performance. Ahmad al-Jack was the promoter of Umm Kulthum’s concerts in Syria and Lebanon, from the early 1930s to the mid 1960s. He is said to have given Umm Kulthum the title of “Kawkab Al-Sharq” or “Star of The East”. The venue is often, erroneously, said to have been the house of Ahmad al-Jack in Beirut but it was, in fact, The Capitol Hotel in Beirut. Umm Kulthum was to have performed at a ceremony in celebration of the marriage of Prince Talal bin Abdulaziz Al Saud and Princess Muna Al Solh, the daughter of Riad Al Solh, the first Prime Minister of Lebanon. The marriage had actually taken place the previous month but a special ceremony was due to have been held on 22nd October 1954 in Beirut. However, Prince Talal received a telegram from his brother, King Saud bin Abdulaziz Al Saud, in which he is said to have stated 'singing is contrary to our traditions’. Umm Kulthum, therefore was not permitted to perform at the ceremony. The Musicians’ Syndicate of Lebanon decided to hold an alternative ceremony honouring Umm Kulthum. This took place at The Capitol Hotel and was attended by the then Lebanese Prime Minister, Sami El Solh, the President of The Musicians’ Syndicate of Lebanon, Saber Al-Safah, Sheikh Mubarak Abdullah Al-Jaber Al-Sabah from Kuwait, and many writers and people from the arts in Lebanon. The performance was recorded by Radio Lebanon.",
