   "eventType": "Religious Performance / Patronage Encounter / Early Career Milestone",
   "people": "Umm Kulthum\nShaykh Ibrāhīm al-Sayyid (her father)\nShaykh Abū al-ʿIlā Muḥammad (mentor)\nʿIzz al-Dīn Yakan Bey (later Pasha)\nEstate overseer (intermediary)\nIsmāʿīl Sukkar (religious singer)",
   "works": "Recitation of the Qiṣṣat al-Nabī (Story/Life of the Prophet Mohamed) for Laylat al-Miʿrāj",
   "locationInfo": "Helwan was a southern suburb of Cairo known in the early twentieth century for elite residences, health resorts, and seasonal palaces. It functioned as an interface between rural estates and the capital, hosting major landowning families. Such residences played a key role in introducing provincial performers to Cairo’s elite audiences.",
   "description": "The residence of ʿIzz al-Dīn Yakan Bey in Helwan represents one of Umm Kulthum’s earliest documented encounters with elite Cairo society and an important precursor to her eventual move to the capital in late 1923–early 1924 with her mentor Shaykh Abū al-ʿIlā Muḥammad. Her appearance there on Laylat al-Miʿrāj (26 Rajab 1335 / May 19, 1917) was arranged through the overseer of the Yakan family’s rural estates near Tammay al-Zahayra, illustrating the vital role of estate networks in connecting rural entertainers to urban patrons.\nA formal written contract, negotiated by her father, stipulated a fee of 3 Egyptian pounds plus expenses, reflecting early professionalization and mobility. Upon arrival, ʿIzz al-Dīn Yakan was reportedly shocked by Umm Kulthum’s youth and initially ordered a replacement singer, relegating her to the servants’ quarters. Later that evening, after the main festivities, he requested that she sing; her performance marked a successful entry into elite patronage circles.\nSuch events were crucial stepping stones in Umm Kulthum’s career. Through elite residences like the Yakan palace in Helwan, she gained access to influential audiences, higher fees, and reputational capital. These early patronage encounters, mediated by written contracts and overseers, laid the groundwork for her increasing appearances in Cairo and her eventual permanent relocation to the city in the early 1920s.",
