   "locationInfo": "The Cecil Hotel in Alexandria, located at 16 Saad Zaghloul Square overlooking the Corniche (31.2008°N, 29.8984°E), is a historic four-star hotel built in 1929 by the French-Egyptian Jewish Metzger family on the site where Cleopatra’s Needles once stood. Over the decades, it hosted prominent figures such as Somerset Maugham, Winston Churchill, and Al Capone, and appeared in major literary works including The Alexandria Quartet by Lawrence Durrell and Miramar by Naguib Mahfouz. Following Egypt’s 1952 revolution, the hotel was nationalized and the Metzger family later expelled, though ownership was legally restored in 2007 before being sold to the Egyptian government. Formerly operated as the Sofitel Cecil Alexandria Hotel, it joined the Steigenberger Hotels chain in 2014, and in December 2023, Icon Company acquired a 51% stake in Legacy Hotels, the company that owns the hotel.",
   "description": "According to oral interviews and testimonies, Umm Kulthum regularly stayed at the Cecil Hotel during the 1940s and 1950s. Her stays were either summer holidays or periods coinciding with her performances in Alexandria, most notably at the Alhambra Theatre. Accounts describe her lifestyle at the hotel as modest; she ate lightly and was known to have her coffee at a fixed, specific time every day.",
