   "works": "Not specified",
   "locationInfo": "Ras El-Bar, located at the mouth of the Damietta Nile branch on the Mediterranean coast, is a historic resort city in Egypt’s Damietta Governorate. Known as the “Cape of the Land,” it has long served as a summer retreat, attracting both local and international visitors with its sandy beaches, seaside villas, and vibrant cultural life. Originally developed in the late 19th and early 20th centuries with simple seasonal huts called “ʿeshsh,” the city evolved into a hub of tourism and leisure, featuring hotels, restaurants, theaters, and recreational facilities. Beyond its role as a resort, Ras El-Bar has been an important port for fishing and trade, contributing to the Nile Delta’s economy, while its unique peninsula geography, wildlife, and coastal dunes make it both a cultural and natural landmark. The city’s historical significance is enhanced by its association with famous Egyptian artists and intellectuals who frequented its hotels and villas, making it a central site in the social and artistic life of the 20th century.",
   "description": "Overlooking the nile branch and not far from the sea branch, Marin Fouad Hotel was one of Ras El-Bar’s most famous hotels attracting wealthy visitors and artists during the 1920s–1940s. Umm Kulthum frequently stayed here and reportedly preferred it over other hotels, although it is unconfirmed whether she had a private “ʿesha” (hut) on the grounds. Many such huts were characteristic of Ras El-Bar’s early seaside architecture, but most—including any that may have belonged to Umm Kulthum—have likely been demolished. The hotel and the surrounding area were a social hub where prominent figures of Egyptian arts and letters gathered. She also performed here several times mostly as private concerts during her holidays.",
