   "date": "Foundation stone: 18 June 1973",
   "accuracy": "confirmed",
   "eventType": "Charitable initiative / unrealized project",
   "people": "Umm Kulthum; Fakri Abaza; Saleh Gawdat; Anis Mansour; Hassan Zaki (Bank of Cairo); Hamdy Ashour (Governor of Cairo); Osman Ahmed Osman; Mahmoud Awad; Jehan Sadat (contextual)",
   "works": "N/A",
   "locationInfo": "According to one of her last radio interviews in late 1973, she mentioned that it will be located near to the fontana on the nile (Hilton Cairo Grand Nile today)",
   "description": "In 1972, Umm Kulthum conceived a charitable project bearing her name, originally intended as a modest home to shelter and train orphaned girls in embroidery and sewing, providing them with both residence and livelihood. Over time, and amid consultations with politicians, businessmen, and intellectuals, the project expanded conceptually to include a theater, museum, recording studios, rehearsal halls, a concert hall, cinema, and later even a luxury hotel intended to fund the complex. The foundation stone was laid on 18 June 1973, and Umm Kulthum personally donated large sums, including cheques totaling tens of thousands of Egyptian pounds. Despite official approval and a designated site, the project stalled and was never completed. Contemporary accounts, press reports, and later memoirs cite bureaucratic delays, financial and political obstacles, and alleged rivalry with other state-backed philanthropic initiatives—most notably the Al-Wafaa wal-Amal Association led by Jehan El-Sadat, wife of President Anwar El-Sadat—as contributing factors. Jehan El-Sadat, however, publicly rejected these claims on several occasions after Umm Kulthum’s death, denying any role in obstructing the project. Alongside these controversies, Umm Kulthum’s declining health and gradual retirement in the final years of her life further complicated the project’s realization. She died in 1975 with the project not fully realized (only a small part of the plan was realized), marking what many describe as her last unfulfilled dream.",
