   "people": "– Umm Kulthum\n– Ahmed Fouad Negm (poet)",
   "works": "“كلب الست” (Kalb El-Sett) — satirical poem by Ahmed Fouad Negm inspired directly by this event",
   "locationInfo": "A prominent villa located on El Gabalaya Street in Zamalek, overlooking the Nile. This residence was one of Umm Kulthum’s known private homes during the 1940s–1960s and was situated near the Fine Arts district and the former Zamalek sports and academic complexes.",
   "description": "At this villa’s entrance on El Gabalaya Street occurred the dog-bite incident that later became one of the most famous humorous stories in Egyptian pop culture. A student named Ismail, walking near the villa during an inter-college sports day, was bitten by Umm Kulthum’s fox terrier (“فوكس”). A police report and medical examination followed, but Umm Kulthum and the dog were exempted from responsibility due to her national standing. The incident, tied directly to this location, inspired Ahmed Fouad Negm to write the celebrated satirical poem Kalb El-Sett, which vividly describes Zamalek, the villa’s surroundings, and the social disparities of the time.",
