   "locationInfo": "BBC Broadcasting House in London is the headquarters of the British Broadcasting Corporation and the long-standing home of the BBC Arabic Service, established in the 1930s. It became an essential platform for Arab-world communication before and after WWII. The arabic services of the BBC stopped in 2022 due to financial issues after eight decades of communication.",
   "description": "In 1949, during a formal visit to London in the era of King Farouk I, Umm Kulthum—then serving as President of the Artists’ Syndicate—was hosted by the BBC Arabic Service. To mark her presence in the British capital, the BBC invited her to deliver a special radio address to the Egyptian people.\nIn her message, Umm Kulthum greeted “my dear fellow citizens” and expressed gratitude to the BBC for giving her the opportunity to speak to listeners from London. She highlighted the role of Egyptian artists in supporting society and charitable causes and emphasized the guidance and encouragement they received from King Farouk.\nDecades later, BBC broadcaster Fouad Abdel Razek rediscovered the recording while preparing his archival program “Memory of Radio”, calling it a rare gem—remarking that Umm Kulthum spoke to the Egyptian people “as though she were a queen upon the throne of art,” sending her greetings from London to “the patron of art and artists,” King Farouk.",
