   "locationInfo": "Alhambra Cinema was a historic cultural venue in downtown Alexandria, originally opened as a theater in 1900 and later converted to a cinema by the late 1920s. It served as a popular location for local and international film screenings for much of the 20th century. The building was part of Alexandria’s vibrant downtown cinematic culture and close to the main tram line, making it accessible to residents and visitors alike. It closed by the late 1990s and no longer functions as a cinema.",
   "description": "This performance was part of a charity concert organized by the Sons of El Sharkia Association at Cinema Alhambra, Alexandria, on 20 December 1962. While the exact program of the concert is not fully documented, it is highly probable that the pieces Ansak (انساك) and La Ya Habibi (لا ياحبيبى) were also performed alongside Hasibak Lil-Zaman (حسيبك للزمن), which has only very recently been discovered. This rendition of Hasibak Lil-Zaman is also known as Mouch Momken Abadan and is distinct from the 11 other known recordings of the piece. The performance does not appear to originate from the Qasr Al-Nil, Cairo concert on 7 June 1962, which remains unavailable. Based on known concert patterns from 1962, the attribution of the other two pieces to this concert is considered highly probable. The recording’s audio quality is poor, but it represents a rare and significant addition to Umm Kulthum’s documented performances in Alexandria during the period 7 December 1961 to 1963.",
