   "people": "Umm Kulthum\nTalaat Harb (founder of Studio Misr)\nAhmed Badrakhan (director)\nTogo Mizrahi (producer/director)",
   "works": "Films (Umm Kulthum, 1936–1947):\nWedad (وداد) – 1936\nNashid al-Amal (نشيد الأمل) – 1937\nDananir (دنانير) – 1940\nSalama (سلامة) – 1945\nFatma (فاطمة) – 1947",
   "locationInfo": "Studio Misr was established in Cairo as part of a national industrial and cultural project. It served as a central production hub equipped with sound stages, laboratories, and training facilities, enabling large-scale Egyptian film production independent of foreign studios.",
   "description": "Studio Misr, founded in 1935 by nationalist economist Talaat Harb, played a foundational role in the creation of modern Egyptian cinema. Conceived as part of a broader project of economic and cultural independence, the studio professionalized film production in Egypt and transformed cinema into a major vehicle of national culture.\nBetween 1936 and 1947, Studio Misr produced all six of Umm Kulthum’s films, carefully shaping her screen persona and extending her musical influence beyond live performance and radio. Beginning with Wedad (1936), One of Egypt’s first major musical film, and concluding with Fatma (1947), these films blended music, melodrama, and historical or moral narratives, reinforcing Umm Kulthum’s image as both a national icon and a moral figure.\nStudio Misr’s collaboration with Umm Kulthum was central to the studio’s success and to the legitimization of musical cinema in Egypt. Her films set aesthetic and commercial standards, helped consolidate Arabic-language cinema as a mass medium, and positioned Egyptian cinema as the dominant film industry in the Arab world. Through Studio Misr, cinema became an extension of Egypt’s broader cultural modernity project, linking music, nationalism, and visual culture.",
