   "accuracy": "High for event; approximate for specific day",
   "eventType": "Private literary gathering; artistic salon performance; poet–singer collaboration",
   "people": "Umm Kulthum\nAhmad Shawqi\nEgyptian literati present at the gathering\nThe song is Composed by Riad al-Sunbati (and the band)",
   "works": "Salu Ku'us Al-Tila",
   "locationInfo": "Ahmed Shawki Museum, formerly the poet’s residence known as Karmet Ibn Hani, is located on the Nile Corniche in Giza, part of Greater Cairo. Originally built by Shawki after his return from exile, the residence takes the form of a lavish white palace surrounded by a green garden. The building now functions as a writer’s house museum, officially opened on 17 June 1977, and preserves Shawki’s personal belongings, manuscripts, and memorabilia.\nThe exterior features bronze statues, including a large replica of Shawki created by sculptor Gamal El-Sagini, representing the poet’s cultural stature; the garden includes cherubic torchbearers symbolizing enlightenment.\nThe interior retains the original character of a private literary salon. On the ground floor, the Mohammed Abdel Wahab Suite houses Shawki’s library with 332 books, hand-written drafts of his poems, and an audio archive of recordings, including material linking Shawki to Umm Kulthum’s early performances of his poetry.\nThe upper floor includes Shawki’s bedroom, his wife Khadija Hanem Shaheen’s quarters, a study with over 713 manuscripts, oil paintings, antiques, and a room displaying his awards, certificates, insignias, and gala uniform. The building embodies the intersection of literary, cultural, and musical heritage in Egypt, marking a site where Umm Kulthum performed and engaged with the country’s leading literary figures.",
   "description": "At Ahmed Shawqi’s home, Young Umm Kulthum once sang before the poet and a gathering of Egyptian literati. Moved by her performance, Shawqi sent her his poem “Salu Ku’us al-Tila” the next day as a personal tribute. The original text included lines directly addressing Umm Kulthum by name. When she later chose to set the poem to music after Shawqi’s death on 14 October 1932, the lines referring to her personally were removed, and later editions of the poem appear without these dedications. This event marks one of the most significant intersections between Umm Kulthum and Egypt’s literary elite.",
