   "people": "Umm Kulthum (Performer), Mohammad al-Qasabji (Composer), Zakariya Ahmad (Composer), Riad al-Sunbati (Composer), Bayram al-Tunsi (Poet), Ahmad Ramy (Poet), King Farouq I of Egypt (Honorary attendee), Ahmed Hassanein Pasha (Head of Royal Court), Mustafa Amin (Journalist)",
   "works": "Raqq Il-Habib, Ana Fi Intizarak, Ya Laylit Il-'Id",
   "locationInfo": "Founded in 1907, Al-Ahly Sports Club is one of Cairo’s most prestigious and historically significant venues, and among the most well-known and acclaimed clubs in the Global South. Originally established as a center for sports, social gatherings, and cultural events, it became a key site for high-profile concerts and public celebrations. The club was known for hosting elite members of Egyptian society, including royalty, government officials, and leading artists. Its halls and gardens were frequently used for musical performances and social functions, making it a symbol of cultural prestige and national pride.",
   "description": "This concert is one of Umm Kulthum’s most celebrated and historically significant performances. It is her earliest surviving complete concert. The program featured four pieces, including the earliest surviving performance of Raqq Il-Habib, with a distinctive introduction \"Zikrayati\" by Mohammad al-Qasabji. Ana Fi Intizarak was performed with adaptation to omit the last verse due to King Farouq’s presence. The concert celebrated Eid (19 September 1944). King Farouq made a surprise appearance and bestowed the prestigious female Egyptian Order of Knighthood (‘Nishan al-Kamal’) upon Umm Kulthum, announced to the audience by journalist Mustafa Amin. This was unusual for someone from her humble background. The performance of Raqq Il-Habib here is noted for being the only surviving version with al-Qasabji’s introduction. Many earlier and later performances, including premieres, are not available but we are sure that there was another concert in the same venue in 1949",
