   "works": "Hajartak, Qissat Al-Sadd, Fit Al-Ma'ad, Aghadan Alqak",
   "locationInfo": "The Grand Celebrations Hall at Cairo University, inaugurated in 1935 and covering 3,160 m², features a remarkable hemispherical cupola with windows that provide natural illumination. Its entrance foyer, harmoniously designed with the hall, hosts cultural seminars, art exhibitions, and university events. The hall spans three levels—the main hall, first floor, and second floor—together accommodating nearly 4,000 spectators, with the main hall alone holding 1,099 seats, including 148 equipped for simultaneous translation in seven languages. The stage measures 20 × 20 m², with a professional orchestra hall below, and the hall includes two elaborately designed bannouras to enhance aesthetics and acoustics. Over the decades, the hall has welcomed countless renowned artists, musicians, political leaders, and international dignitaries. Among the most prominent are Umm Kulthum, who gave several landmark concerts here; Egyptian presidents such as Gamal ’Abd al-Nasir, Anwar Sadat, and Hosni Mubarak; and international figures including President Barack Obama in 2009, Kofi Annan, and Ban Ki-moon, as well as distinguished cultural and academic figures, diplomats, and visiting heads of state. The hall remains a prestigious cultural landmark, symbolizing Cairo University’s long-standing commitment to education, arts, and international engagement.",
   "description": "The hall is a key cultural landmark in Cairo, symbolizing the university’s central role in arts, culture, and public life. Throughout the mid-20th century it served as one of Egypt’s most important performance venues, regularly hosting major concerts, national ceremonies, and high-profile regional cultural events. Umm Kulthum appeared on its stage multiple times, giving concerts that further cemented the hall’s status as a nucleus of Egyptian musical life.\nIn 1961, during the annual national celebration known as Eid al-‘Ilm (Festival of Science), the hall hosted one of the most significant cultural ceremonies of the era, where Umm Kulthum was honored alongside Mohamed Abdel-Wahab. During this event she received from President Gamal Abdel Nasser one of the highest grades of the Order of the Republic Medal, a recognition rarely awarded at that level to any Egyptian artist. The ceremony became a defining moment in the relationship between the state and Egypt’s artistic icons, and it reinforced the hall’s reputation as a place where cultural achievement and national prestige converged.",
