   "works": "Misru Tatahaddathu 'An Nafsiha, Ila 'Arafati Illah",
   "locationInfo": "Dar Cinema Radio, located at 24 Talaat Harb Street in Cairo, is one of the city’s most iconic cultural landmarks. Named after the famous Radio City music hall, it was designed by Max Edri in collaboration with Armenian architects Garo Balyan and Victor Salama, with their offices housed in the cinema annex. The cinema complex opened in 1932 with a single large hall featuring the largest screen in Cairo at the time. In the 1970s, the venue was divided into two spaces: a cinema hall and a theater.\nThe hall holds significant cultural importance for hosting Umm Kulthum’s premiere of “Ila 'Arafati Illah” on 6 December 1951, among other historic performances. Its façade and architectural identity were crafted by Hassan Abu Saada, and over the decades, the building also housed well-known shops along its interior passage, including Samira Ahmed’s “Jaljala” and Bisco Misr.\nIn 2009, the Ismailia Real Estate Investment Company acquired the building and initiated phased renovation and adaptive reuse between 2011 and 2017. These works included modernization of façades, main corridors, entrances, commercial areas, administrative offices, and the addition of a panoramic elevator and emergency stairwell, while preserving the hall’s historic character. Today, Dar Cinema Radio continues to serve as a vibrant cultural hub, hosting numerous important events, while also providing commercial and office spaces, sustaining its dual role as a center for culture and business in central Cairo.",
   "description": "On 6 December 1951, Umm Kulthum gave the premiere performances of Misru Tatahaddathu 'An Nafsiha and Ila 'Arafati Illah. The latter included a special section praising King Farouq I, which was later omitted following the 1952 Egyptian Revolution. Dar Cinema Radio was instrumental in introducing patriotic songs and premieres to the Egyptian public and remains a landmark in Cairo’s cultural history.",
