   "locationInfo": "The Egyptian Royal Opera House, also known as the Khedivial Opera House (دار الأوبرا الملكية المصرية / دار الأوبرا الخديوية), was the first opera house in Africa and the Middle East. Commissioned by Khedive Ismail to celebrate the opening of the Suez Canal, it was inaugurated on 1 November 1869 and designed by Italian architect Pietro Avoscani, possibly with assistance from Mario Rossi. Located between the districts of Azbakeya and Ismailiyya—the heart of 19th-century Khedival Cairo—the wooden building seated approximately 850–1,200 people and quickly became the cultural epicenter of Egypt, hosting Verdi’s Rigoletto on its opening night and the world premiere of Aida in 1871. Throughout the 20th century it remained Egypt’s premier venue for opera, ballet, and major concerts, including many of Umm Kulthum’s most important performances. On 28 October 1971, the opera house was destroyed in a devastating fire, leaving only two statues by Mohamed Hassan. Cairo remained without an opera house until the opening of the new Cairo Opera House in 1988, and although the original site is now a multi-story parking garage, the adjacent Opera Square (Mīdān al-Opera) continues to bear the memory of this historic landmark.",
