   "locationInfo": "Qasr Al-Qubba, constructed by Khedive Ismail between 1867 and 1872 on the ruins of a house owned by his father Ibrahim Pasha, is the grandest royal palace of the Muhammad Ali dynasty, stretching over 190 feddans. Named “Qubba” after an older Mamluk-era structure, the palace is renowned for its luxurious gardens, rare groves, and proximity to a lake that historically attracted nobility for promenades and fishing. It hosted numerous royal events, including the weddings of King Fouad I and Queen Nazli (1919), King Farouk and Queen Farida (1938), and the wedding of Princess Fawzia Fuad to Crown Prince Mohammad Reza Pahlavi in 1939, where Umm Kulthum performed. The palace also witnessed King Farouk’s first radio address in 1936 and significant funerary and political events, including the arrival of the Shah of Iran seeking asylum in 1979. Today, Qasr Al-Qubba remains an active presidential palace, continuing to serve as one of Egypt’s official state residences while retaining its historic and ceremonial importance.",
