   "locationInfo": "The Kuttab of Tamay al-Zahayra was a traditional rural Qur’anic school attached to the village mosque. Like most Delta kuttabs of the early 20th century, it was a simple mud-brick structure with mats on the ground, wooden Qur’an stands, and an open-air courtyard. It served as the main educational institution for boys, and only rarely for girls. Her father insisted that she receive formal Qur’anic and Arabic-language education—uncommon at the time for rural girls—believing that knowledge and recitation would strengthen her character and “protect her voice” spiritually and linguistically.",
   "description": "Umm Kulthum’s education at the Kuttab shaped her entire artistic foundation, providing mastery of Classical Arabic, precise articulation, breath control, and emotional delivery rooted in Qur’anic recitation. In her own interviews, she emphasized that her teachers—especially Sheikh Abdel Aziz—taught her correct makhārij al-ḥurūf (pronunciation) and tajwīd, which became the hallmark of her singing and one of the strongest elements of her later career. Her father’s decision to enroll her in the Kuttab was unusual for rural Egypt in the early 1900s, where girls rarely received formal education. This early training enabled her to stand confidently before audiences from an early age and later to interpret complex poetic texts with unmatched precision.",
