   "locationInfo": "Tahrir Square in central Cairo has long stood as one of Egypt’s most powerful symbolic and civic spaces, reflecting the country’s political, social, and cultural transformations across more than a century. Originally laid out in the late 19th century during the Khedival modernization of Cairo and known as Ismailia Square, it became a focal point of national life during the British occupation and was renamed Tahrir (Liberation) Square following the 1952 Revolution. The square has hosted pivotal historical moments, including mass demonstrations, state ceremonies, and landmark cultural events such as Umm Kulthum’s 1954 performance celebrating the Anglo-Egyptian Agreement and the end of British military presence. In modern times, Tahrir Square gained global prominence during the 2011 Egyptian Revolution, when millions gathered there to demand political change, reaffirming its role as a living arena of public expression. From colonial-era planning to revolutionary mobilization and contemporary civic memory, Tahrir Square remains a central symbol of Egyptian identity, sovereignty, and collective voice.",
   "description": "Umm Kulthum performed Sawt al-Watan (Misru Allati Fi Khatiri) in Tahrir Square on 20 October 1954 during the official Evacuation Ceremony marking the signing of the Anglo-Egyptian Agreement, which mandated the withdrawal of British forces from Egypt following more than seven decades of occupation (1882–1952). The performance took place in the presence of state officials and the public as part of celebrations following the 1952 Egyptian Revolution and the proclamation of the Republic. Umm Kulthum personally commissioned this work to mark the revolutionary moment and to publicly demonstrate her support for the new political order and national sovereignty.",
