   "works": "Early works of 1920s",
   "locationInfo": "Al-Mashrah magazine, a prominent cultural and theatrical publication in 1920s Cairo, played a significant role in shaping public discourse around the city’s performing arts and its leading figures. Under the editorship of Abd al-Majid Hilmi, the magazine not only reviewed performances but also reported on the personal lives of artists, blending cultural commentary with sensationalist gossip. Its influence was such that a single article—like the controversial piece about Oum Kalthoum in 1926–1927—could provoke public debate, impact familial decisions, and intensify rivalries between performers, as seen in the tensions between Oum Kalthoum and Mounira al-Mahdiyya. By straddling the line between entertainment journalism and cultural critique, al-Mashrah became an essential platform for both promoting talent and shaping reputations, reflecting the growing power of the press in Cairo’s artistic and social life.",
   "description": "In 1926, after four successful years in Cairo, Oum Kalthoum reorganized her musical ensemble, replacing her father, Sheikh Ibrahim, with top-tier musicians—Sami al-Shawa on violin, Mohammed al-Aqqad on ganun, and Mohammed al-Qasabji on oud—marking her transformation from a rural singer into a refined urban diva. Around this time, she faced rivalry from Mounira al-Mahdiyya, a celebrated veteran singer, who was rumored to have tried to undermine Oum Kalthoum’s rising career, possibly leveraging her relationship with al-Mashrah magazine editor Abd al-Majid Hilmi. In 1926 or 1927, the magazine published a scandalous article about Oum Kalthoum, which reportedly so alarmed her father that he considered taking her out of Cairo. Biographers have suggested that the controversy stemmed from gossip regarding her romantic affairs or the threatened publication of a police report concerning an assault she suffered as a child, though the latter never appeared. Whether incited by a single article or multiple provocations, Oum Kalthoum later attributed the attacks to Mounira. Eventually, friends persuaded her father to let her remain in Cairo, while the press continued to speculate about her love life and unmarried status, often sensationalizing her personal choices and interpreting her independence in ways ranging from playful metaphor to unfounded rumors about her sexuality, reflecting the broader societal fascination with the private lives of prominent female performers.",
