   "locationInfo": "Badiʿa Masabni’s Sala in Cairo was one of the most influential entertainment venues of the 1920s and 1930s, serving as a focal point for musical innovation, rivalry, and the shaping of popular taste. By the late 1920s, the sala had become a key stage for competition among leading female singers, including Umm Kulthum, Fathiyya Ahmad, and Munira al-Mahdiyya. Performances there reflected shifting musical fashions and the emergence of new artistic identities within Egypt’s rapidly evolving urban culture.",
   "description": "In the fall of 1926, the press openly framed Umm Kulthum and Fathiyya Ahmad as rivals after Umm Kulthum hired a full takht, signaling her move into elite urban performance circles. Shortly afterward, Fathiyya Ahmad left Cairo for Syria, amid speculation that she would not return until Umm Kulthum’s success faded. By early 1927, however, Fathiyya returned with renewed strength: new recording and agency contracts and a long-term engagement at Badiʿa Masabni’s Sala, where she performed several nights a week for the next six years. There, she distinguished herself by emphasizing a Turkish-style repertoire and incorporating the mawwāl, a genre Umm Kulthum did not perform.\nAt the same time, Umm Kulthum’s growing success reshaped the competitive landscape. Even established stars such as Munira al-Mahdiyya began incorporating Umm Kulthum’s songs into their concerts, implicitly acknowledging her ability to set musical trends. Badiʿa Masabni’s Sala thus became a central arena in which rivalry, adaptation, and artistic definition played out, marking a crucial moment in the formation of modern Egyptian musical culture.",
