   "locationInfo": "Originally, during the French Protectorate, the site housed the residence of the French Resident-General overseeing the Tunisian government. After independence, President Habib Bourguiba transformed it into the Presidential Palace of Carthage, commissioning its construction between 1960 and 1969 in an Arab-Andalusian architectural style. Since then, the palace has served as the official seat of the Tunisian presidency, witnessing major political transitions before and after the 2011 revolution.",
   "description": "Umm Kulthūm’s historic visit to Tunisia strengthened Egypt-Tunisia cultural ties and contributed to diplomatic normalization. Umm Kulthūm was personally received by President Habib Bourguiba and his wife, awarded the highest grade of the National Order of the Republic, and toured Tunisian cultural sites including markets, the Zitouna Mosque, and the National Distribution Company in Rades. The visit included official banquets, cultural performances, and encounters with local artists and poets, highlighting her influence on Arab cultural identity and public morale after the 1967 defeat.",
